WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.199
Movie sequels are impossible to predict. Some
become classics and some become the most hated

00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:23.009
movies of all time. So what makes a sequel
good, and why do sequels have such a hard

00:00:23.009 --> 00:00:31.089
time not sucking? Let’s compare similar
sequels to see what works and what doesn’t.

00:00:31.089 --> 00:00:34.480
One of the biggest obstacles with a great sequel
is that you have to do something new. Maybe

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.270
the first movie worked great, but the sequel
can’t be the same thing or it’ll suck.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.680
The sequels that we all love mix it up and
surprise us.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.290
The first Terminator features Arnold Schwarzenegger
as the villain, and he’s such a terrifying

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:47.690
villain because he’s not even human. He’s
a robotic monster whose only objective is

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:51.650
to kill Sarah Connor. A lazy, unsurprising
sequel would put us in the same world with

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:55.730
the Terminator trying to kill again, but James
Cameron surprises us, and in a spectacular

00:00:55.730 --> 00:01:00.250
twist, Arnold is actually sent to protect
John Connor from another, more powerful Terminator.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.980
Now that’s a great sequel.
And then, there’s Speed. The first Speed

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:10.300
is about a cop who tries to rescue civilians
from a bus rigged to explode if any of the

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.240
civilians try to get off or the bus slows
down. It’s action packed and suspenseful

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.050
from start to finish. The sequel, Speed 2,
has the same general idea, but the bus is

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:22.140
now a luxury cruise ship. The surprise is
amazingly lame. A slow-moving cruise ship

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.970
is way less thrilling than the fast moving
bus in the first Speed. The surprise isn’t

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.700
a surprise. It’s a let down.
Sequels also need to build. They need to add

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.510
something, and what they add has to be good.
22 Jump Street has the same general plot as

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:37.250
21 Jump Street. The only real difference between
the two films is that one takes place in high

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:41.140
school and the other in college. But the second
film adds a whole gag about the movie itself.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.810
They constantly make references to how doing
the same thing over and over again will not

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:51.420
lead to the same results. They’re making
fun of the fact that sequels never work. They

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:56.200
build on 21 Jump Street by now making fun
of themselves. It’s clever, original, unique, and hilarious.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.340
The Hangover: Part II tries to build on the

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:11.480
original. Instead of taking place in Las Vegas,
the sequel is in the much more exotic Thailand.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.700
But that’s really the only addition to the
original. There’s nothing new here. There’s no build.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.160
An inherent problem with feature-length film

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.670
is that the amount of possible character development
is limited to the hour and a half length of

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:27.440
the movie. So in a medium like television
we can see so many complex changes in a character

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.590
over the course of several seasons whereas
a movie can show maybe one or two major changes.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.730
With a sequel, though, we have a chance to
see characters we love in the original grow

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:38.290
even more. I think any sequel that doesn’t
take advantage of this opportunity for more

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:42.060
character growth is a disappointment. Toy
Story, for example, shows us a whole new character

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.670
arc for Woody in the sequel. In the first
film, Woody makes the realization that getting

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.050
the most attention isn’t what’s important:

00:02:57.459 --> 00:03:00.780
And then, in the sequel, Woody has another
realization that, while his time with Andy

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:06.160
is temporary, he would rather spend his limited time making Andy happy over being displayed in a museum.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.700
Through the sequel, we get further
insight into Woody and his dedication to Andy.

00:03:14.709 --> 00:03:18.459
We learn more about Woody’s goals, his fears,
and his obstacles. The sequel added just as

00:03:18.459 --> 00:03:22.370
much depth to the main character as the first
film which, in the end, is what turns sequels

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:26.470
into classics. We can see this added depth
to characters in other great sequels like

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:29.959
The Godfather: Part II with Michael Corleone
and The Dark Knight with Bruce Wayne.

00:03:29.959 --> 00:03:33.400
When we compare these sequels to something
like Cars 2 and The Hobbit franchise, we see

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:37.269
another way movie sequels can suck. Cars 2
doesn’t show any change to the characters

00:03:37.269 --> 00:03:41.260
from the original, and even with almost eight
hours of screen time for The Hobbit series,

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:44.290
we see hardly any character depth in most
of the ensemble cast.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.010
I have to commend the Marvel Cinematic Universe
for knowing how to make great sequels. You

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:52.129
can tell from their consistent continuity,
writing style, crossovers, and brand marketing,

00:03:52.129 --> 00:03:55.780
that Disney isn’t making sequels just for
one-time cash grabs. Disney is attempting

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:59.680
to make very long-term profit by creating
a lasting franchise and brand, and it seems

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.069
to be working. Their sequels surprise, build,
and add character depth. Think about The Avengers.

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:07.409
We get loads of surprises when our favorite
super heroes fight it out, build on plots

00:04:07.409 --> 00:04:11.409
from previous films, and we see tons of character
depth by seeing the conflicts of interest

00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:14.659
between each super hero. I’m not saying
Marvel movies are the greatest movies of all

00:04:14.659 --> 00:04:18.000
time, but what I am saying is that Marvel
knows how to make sequels, and that’s saying

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.440
a lot given the countless disappointments
we’ve seen in sequels.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.060
Making a good sequel to a great original is
definitely a challenge, but I think a big

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:29.440
problem with sequels is that a lot of the
time the studio doesn’t really care if a

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:33.690
sequel is well reviewed. With a successful
original, as long as a sequel is OK, it’s

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:37.090
almost guaranteed to make a profit for the
studio. Remember that despite all the hate

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:40.970
towards sequels like Cars 2, The Hangover:
Part II, and the Star Wars prequels, they

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:44.780
grossed more than their original films. Sequels
create an interesting paradox in the film

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:48.850
industry. To be successful, a film normally
has to show something unique like great special

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:52.790
effects, acting, or plot, but a sequel can
make a profit only by having a recognizable

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:57.450
name and providing nothing unique. So do Hollywood
a favor and stop going to sequels that suck.

